  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Monthly Statement",
    "overview": "FundFlow reads like a calm, banking-grade statement of the month. Two cool neutral layers carry everything: a white content plane resting inside a slightly cooler grey sidebar. Ink is a navy-tinted near-black. A single indigo accent does four jobs only (primary action, current selection, focus, and the pace line), so wherever it appears, it means something. Everything else is hairlines, soft low shadows and an 8px radius.\n\nDensity is that of a working financial tool: 14px body text, 32px controls, 16 to 24px gutters, hairline-divided lists and summary cells. Numbers are the content. Amounts are exact, set in Geist with tabular figures wherever they sit in a column, and they are never decorative. Colour that is not the accent or a neutral is either state (green on track, amber ahead of pace, red over) or category identity (a small swatch beside a category name). The system rejects the colourful, gamified budgeting-app look and the old pink-and-purple gradients outright.\n\nThe one signature is the pace line: a 2px indigo hairline drawn across every variable budget bar at today's point in the month, ringed in the page colour so it reads as a mark in the bar rather than part of the fill. Spending left of it is on pace; a fill that runs past it turns amber before it ever turns red. The landing page uses the same world and the same components with labelled sample data, and its one authored motion is those bars filling in toward an already-placed pace line.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two cool neutral planes (white content, cooler grey sidebar), navy-tinted ink, full light and dark themes in OKLCH.",
      "One indigo accent, reserved for primary action, current selection, focus and the pace line.",
      "Green, amber and red appear only as state; category hues only as identity swatches.",
      "Geist as the only face; tabular figures for amounts in columns.",
      "1px hairlines, two soft shadows, 8px controls and 11px containers.",
      "The pace line on every variable budget bar."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Indigo Rule",
        "body": "The accent is reserved for the primary action, the current selection, focus and the pace line. Nothing else is indigo, and there is one primary button per view.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The State Only Rule",
        "body": "Green, amber and red appear only where they carry state: on track, ahead of pace, over. A neutral fill (ink at 70%) is the on-track default; it is not green.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Swatch Beside a Name Rule",
        "body": "Category hues appear only as identity swatches beside the category name, drawn from the validated eight-slot palette. They never fill a bar, tint a row or stand alone.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ordinal Ink Rule",
        "body": "Every categorical hue belongs to a category, so non-category series (Income and Spent) are two shades of one ink hue, never two categorical colours.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Column Rule",
        "body": "Every amount that sits in a column, row or status line uses tabular, lining figures (the `num` utility or `tabular-nums`) so digits line up. Large standalone stat values keep proportional figures.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Face Rule",
        "body": "Geist is the only typeface. Hierarchy comes from size and weight (400, 500, 600), never from a second family, all-caps or letter-spaced labels.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Hairline First Rule",
        "body": "Separation comes from 1px hairlines and tonal planes. Reach for Soft only to seat a plane, and for Lift only on something that genuinely floats.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do keep one primary button per view, and use the indigo accent only for that action, the current selection, focus and the pace line.",
      "Do colour a budget only by state: ink at 70% on track, amber ahead of pace, red over, with the state repeated in a 12px text line under the bar.",
      "Do draw the pace line on every variable budget bar, and omit it for fixed bills, which read \"Fixed bill, flagged only if over\".",
      "Do set amounts in columns and status lines with tabular figures; keep large stat values proportional.",
      "Do pair every chart with a screen-reader table and a labelled figure.",
      "Do label sample data as sample wherever product fragments appear outside the signed-in app.",
      "Do separate with 1px hairlines and tonal planes before reaching for a shadow."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use a category hue anywhere except as a swatch beside that category's name.",
      "Don't give a non-category series (Income, Spent) a categorical colour; use the ordinal ink pair.",
      "Don't use green, amber or red decoratively or as an accent.",
      "Don't invent social proof: no testimonials, user counts, ratings, press or savings figures.",
      "Don't bring back gradients, the pink-and-purple palette, confetti or other gamified budgeting-app flourishes.",
      "Don't add a second typeface, all-caps labels or letter-spaced eyebrows above headings.",
      "Don't animate beyond the landing's bar fill-in and short state transitions, and honour reduced motion."
